          "p": "John Stuart Mill (1806 to 1873) was raised as an experiment. His father, James Mill, a disciple of Bentham, decided to grow a reasoning machine and started early: by his own later account, John was learning Greek at three and Latin at eight, doing political economy (the study of how wealth is produced and distributed, what we now call economics) as a boy the way other children do chores. (That account comes from Mill's own *Autobiography*, a carefully shaped book, so read it as his telling rather than a transcript.) The experiment produced a brilliant young utilitarian and then, when Mill was twenty, a total breakdown. He asked himself one morning whether, if every social reform he was fighting for were achieved tomorrow, he would actually be happy, and the honest answer was no. The pleasure-calculus he had been raised inside had no room for the question. The breakdown told him something Bentham's system could not hold: that what *kind* of happiness matters, not just how much of it you can stack up. Climbing out of that was the work of his life, and it pulled utilitarianism into a richer shape."
